        "original_id": "9a29a2c6-9096-579b-94f7-8c9c1b25d3fd",
        "text": "This chunk describes the High Tider dialect and a land-loss fact about Tangier, Virginia.\n\nThis chunk is about:\n- Concepts: High Tider, Hoi Toider, American English dialects\n- Places: South Atlantic United States, North Carolina Down East, Outer Banks, Pamlico Sound, Carteret County, Atlantic, Sea Level, Harkers Island, Ocracoke, Chesapeake Bay, Tangier, Smith Island, Tangier, Virginia\n\nFacts:\n- Since 1850, Tangier, Virginia, has lost 67% of its landmass.\n- High Tider, also called \"Hoi Toider,\" is a dialect of American English.\n- High Tider is spoken in very limited communities in the South Atlantic United States.\n- High Tider is spoken in small island and coastal townships of North Carolina's rural Down East region, including Atlantic, Sea Level, Harkers Island, and Ocracoke.\n- High Tider is also spoken in Chesapeake Bay communities such as Tangier and Smith Island.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
